85
00:31:25,750 --> 00:31:29,190
So first a bit about the Biodiversity meets Data project, which is quite an

86
00:31:29,230 --> 00:31:32,810
ambitious project, with the idea to build a whole pipeline going from a data

87
00:31:32,870 --> 00:31:36,850
catalog of different resources to a set of analytical tools that are

88
00:31:36,910 --> 00:31:40,790
easy to use for downstream users, in particular managers of Natura 2000 sites.

89
00:31:41,650 --> 00:31:45,350
For all of that to work, there needs to be a whole ecosystem making these analyses

90
00:31:45,410 --> 00:31:48,970
work. One of the key components is a central harmonization layer,

91
00:31:49,350 --> 00:31:53,050
which takes data from the catalog, harmonizes it to, for instance, same

92
00:31:53,090 --> 00:31:56,910
squared reference system, but also other transformations, and feeds

93
00:31:56,970 --> 00:32:00,950
those to, that's the idea in the end, an API to the analytical

94
00:32:01,010 --> 00:32:02,190
tools. So

95
00:32:03,190 --> 00:32:06,990
to make that work, we need quite a bit of technical

96
00:32:07,050 --> 00:32:09,390
setup to optimize this and to make it scalable.

97
00:32:11,870 --> 00:32:15,550
For that, we also wanted to build on the tools developed in the D3

98
00:32:15,830 --> 00:32:19,590
project, which recently finished, and in which an extra

99
00:32:19,670 --> 00:32:23,570
API was developed for GBIF, which facilitates exporting

100
00:32:23,610 --> 00:32:27,590
data into a resampled manner called cubes, which also makes them

101
00:32:27,870 --> 00:32:28,610
safer to use,

102
00:32:29,470 --> 00:32:33,050
considering that they often have very heterogeneous data in the background.

103
00:32:37,250 --> 00:32:41,090
So this is what it looks like, the more or

104
00:32:41,130 --> 00:32:42,430
less architectural structure

105
00:32:43,250 --> 00:32:47,030
of the cubing engine. So there's a considerable back end, which is as

106
00:32:47,070 --> 00:32:49,450
modular as possible, and a very slim and

107
00:32:50,510 --> 00:32:53,910
as tiny as possible front end to make it as easy to use as possible.

108
00:32:53,970 --> 00:32:57,830
I'm going to go into a bit more depth into both in the

109
00:32:57,870 --> 00:33:01,670
next few slides, but not into too much detail because there's not that much

110
00:33:02,210 --> 00:33:05,970
time in this presentation. So first, the back end, as modular as

111
00:33:06,030 --> 00:33:09,970
possible. Central to it is this base grid class, which lists the

112
00:33:10,010 --> 00:33:12,690
currently supported grids and coordinate reference systems.

113
00:33:13,110 --> 00:33:15,090
It could be extended in the future for more.

114
00:33:15,390 --> 00:33:17,090
Currently, it supports the

115
00:33:18,010 --> 00:33:20,890
EEA grid and the Global Eco Area one and

116
00:33:21,430 --> 00:33:22,190
WGS84.

117
00:33:23,050 --> 00:33:27,030
Then very close to that are the raster and vector engines, which are really

118
00:33:27,090 --> 00:33:30,730
geospatial classes intended to just optimize and

119
00:33:31,010 --> 00:33:34,810
just focus on the geospatial transformations like snapping to grids and

120
00:33:34,850 --> 00:33:38,810
reprojecting needed for the harmonization, completely abstracted from the

121
00:33:38,870 --> 00:33:39,950
different data sources.

122
00:33:40,930 --> 00:33:44,490
Then we have the data cube orchestrator, which orchestrates the different

123
00:33:44,510 --> 00:33:48,390
harmonization processes with different data sources that are needed and has as

124
00:33:48,470 --> 00:33:52,070
abstract child classes, this raster and vector cube

125
00:33:52,130 --> 00:33:53,350
class, which

126
00:33:54,370 --> 00:33:55,670
are abstract classes. And

127
00:33:56,730 --> 00:34:00,290
as child classes of those, we have classes for each different data

128
00:34:00,350 --> 00:34:04,130
source that the cubing engine currently supports, making it very easy to

129
00:34:04,170 --> 00:34:07,970
extend because in principle, all you have to do is create another child

130
00:34:08,031 --> 00:34:11,990
class, which indicates the logic needed to access the data and to convert it

131
00:34:12,031 --> 00:34:14,750
to the central data model that the cubing engine expects.

132
00:34:16,611 --> 00:34:18,190
In the front end, we have

133
00:34:19,150 --> 00:34:22,890
one main class called BMDCube, and in principle, what it mainly is

134
00:34:22,930 --> 00:34:26,650
intended to do is read a markup language file, a YAML

135
00:34:26,730 --> 00:34:26,950
file,

136
00:34:27,890 --> 00:34:31,790
which includes the recipe of what the cubing engine is supposed to do in terms of

137
00:34:31,810 --> 00:34:35,270
harmonization, which data sources it's supposed to access, and according to which

138
00:34:35,310 --> 00:34:38,730
dimensions. And in principle, if you feed that YAML

139
00:34:38,810 --> 00:34:42,490
file into this function, and you wait long enough,

140
00:34:42,551 --> 00:34:46,270
depending on how heavy the operation is, you get the output

141
00:34:46,330 --> 00:34:47,310
without minimal

142
00:34:48,310 --> 00:34:52,150
skills in terms of coding notebooks and having to process the code

143
00:34:52,210 --> 00:34:52,910
on your end.

144
00:34:53,710 --> 00:34:57,390
So the output that is created out of this is a data

145
00:34:57,450 --> 00:35:00,370
tree of the harmonized files that you want to have,

146
00:35:01,270 --> 00:35:04,890
split up for raster and vector files, but harmonized to the same

147
00:35:05,070 --> 00:35:05,690
master grid.

148
00:35:06,550 --> 00:35:10,170
There's a stack file, which describes the cube, its internal structure, so it could

149
00:35:10,209 --> 00:35:14,030
be indexed and makes it more easy to find the cube if you develop a large

150
00:35:14,090 --> 00:35:17,150
storage space for them, which is the intention in the Biodiversity meets Data

151
00:35:17,190 --> 00:35:20,850
project, and a provenance record which details, for instance, the

152
00:35:20,910 --> 00:35:24,470
runtime, the version of the cubing engine used, the version of the dependencies

153
00:35:24,570 --> 00:35:27,690
used, and the hardware underneath it.

154
00:35:28,330 --> 00:35:32,310
So all to optimize and to make sure that the whole process is as reusable

155
00:35:32,350 --> 00:35:33,770
and reproducible as possible.

156
00:35:34,970 --> 00:35:38,670
So in principle, this is the minimal line of code that you could use

157
00:35:39,050 --> 00:35:42,870
to launch a cubing engine process and get the cubing engine output in the

158
00:35:42,930 --> 00:35:43,210
end.

159
00:35:45,590 --> 00:35:47,430
And this is an example of how the output looks.

160
00:35:47,450 --> 00:35:50,770
So you have different folders for the different data sources with different files,

161
00:35:50,810 --> 00:35:54,790
depending on how many variables there are, and we have a more complex setup for

162
00:35:54,830 --> 00:35:58,130
vector output. So you have the original vector items, but also harmonized to the

163
00:35:58,170 --> 00:35:59,350
grid. And

164
00:36:00,710 --> 00:36:04,070
you can easily stream those files from a central storage layer, so you don't have

165
00:36:04,110 --> 00:36:07,770
to download the whole cube. If you want it, you can actually be more flexible and

166
00:36:08,150 --> 00:36:10,570
only access the files that you need from this cube.

167
00:36:12,630 --> 00:36:15,270
This is a bit about the software stack.

168
00:36:15,330 --> 00:36:17,010
So the whole tool is built in Python.

169
00:36:17,430 --> 00:36:20,670
It obviously uses some of the common geospatial libraries,

170
00:36:21,130 --> 00:36:25,050
such as GDAL for the geospatial processing, GeoPandas to

171
00:36:25,130 --> 00:36:28,890
store the objects internally in memory, xarray as the internal central

172
00:36:28,950 --> 00:36:32,550
data model, and Dask and/or DuckDB for

173
00:36:32,630 --> 00:36:36,510
optimized processing, in particular for very large files, which tends to

174
00:36:36,570 --> 00:36:38,910
happen with some Earth observation data.

175
00:36:39,890 --> 00:36:42,090
You can see a link to the repository there as well.

176
00:36:42,550 --> 00:36:46,270
And currently for output, we support GeoPark for the vector files

177
00:36:46,350 --> 00:36:49,330
and NetCDF or Zarr for the raster files.

178
00:36:50,486 --> 00:36:54,286
And not importantly, in the current version of the Cubing Engine, we

179
00:36:54,326 --> 00:36:57,986
mainly support data coming from GBIF, from Celsa climate data,

180
00:36:58,546 --> 00:37:00,446
and earth observation data coming through by GEO.

181
00:37:01,326 --> 00:37:04,786
Though we're currently adjusting that pipeline to work directly with the S3 bucket

182
00:37:04,866 --> 00:37:07,626
storage from through Copernicus to make it more

183
00:37:09,246 --> 00:37:10,846
streamlined, and we don't have to bother with

184
00:37:11,886 --> 00:37:14,826
accessing it through the API. We can just access the files directly.

185
00:37:18,166 --> 00:37:18,466
So

186
00:37:19,346 --> 00:37:23,006
finally, I want to go then into a bit more detail on this YAML

187
00:37:23,066 --> 00:37:25,086
recipe file and what it looks like.

188
00:37:25,866 --> 00:37:27,686
So it includes configuration

189
00:37:28,526 --> 00:37:28,726
for

190
00:37:29,626 --> 00:37:33,506
the global configuration, which is mainly just where you

191
00:37:33,566 --> 00:37:35,946
want the output to go, what format you want it to go.

192
00:37:36,686 --> 00:37:40,046
The spatial configuration, so what is the target width, at which

193
00:37:40,106 --> 00:37:43,746
resolution, if you have a bounding box, if you have a temporal resolution

194
00:37:43,786 --> 00:37:45,346
restriction that you want to apply.

195
00:37:46,646 --> 00:37:49,466
And you have some more flexibility, as you can see in the different parameters

196
00:37:49,506 --> 00:37:49,746
here.

197
00:37:50,966 --> 00:37:52,846
Then there are some more data source specific

198
00:37:54,526 --> 00:37:58,186
terms that you can add. These, in principle, need to be

199
00:37:58,246 --> 00:38:01,026
coded into these child classes, which I talked about before.

200
00:38:01,426 --> 00:38:04,966
But oftentimes it's just which variables you want to include, maybe some

201
00:38:05,146 --> 00:38:06,926
specific parameters for these variables.

202
00:38:06,986 --> 00:38:10,886
In this case, as an example of Celsa, it is mainly which models,

203
00:38:10,986 --> 00:38:14,486
which variables, which time ranges for these models that you want to include, which

204
00:38:14,506 --> 00:38:18,346
you need to specify in the recipe. And this may look a bit

205
00:38:18,966 --> 00:38:21,566
complicated, but this is something that you could easily

206
00:38:22,426 --> 00:38:25,546
add a front-end wrapper on top of it with an interface with some form elements.

207
00:38:25,886 --> 00:38:29,106
And so the user would just fill in the form, gets transformed into the YAML file,

208
00:38:29,506 --> 00:38:31,766
fed into the function, and the Cubing Engine would run.

209
00:38:32,986 --> 00:38:35,706
For GBIF, it can get a bit more complicated.

210
00:38:35,726 --> 00:38:37,026
There's also a bit more flexibility.

211
00:38:37,066 --> 00:38:40,006
There's a lot of different additional parameters you could use.

212
00:38:40,926 --> 00:38:44,606
So you can look at different taxon keys that you want to focus on, at which taxon

213
00:38:44,646 --> 00:38:48,106
ranks you want to aggregate them. You can have different parameters for the

214
00:38:48,146 --> 00:38:51,386
uncertainty. You can have a default uncertainty, minimum or maximum.

215
00:38:51,586 --> 00:38:54,386
You can exclude some issues that GBIF flags to the data.

216
00:38:54,826 --> 00:38:56,186
And there's also some options

217
00:38:58,285 --> 00:39:02,006
for vector processing. So as I said very quickly before,

218
00:39:02,926 --> 00:39:06,226
GBIF now supports resampling some of its occurrence data through the SQL

219
00:39:06,306 --> 00:39:08,806
API using some cubing functions.

220
00:39:10,226 --> 00:39:14,026
We also incorporated that logic into the Cubing Engine, but extended it a bit, so

221
00:39:14,066 --> 00:39:17,826
you can now also, through the Cubing Engine, do a more Monte Carlo resampling, so

222
00:39:17,906 --> 00:39:20,126
you have much more resampling of the same point.

223
00:39:20,606 --> 00:39:24,006
And you can also snap that to grid cells at a fractional

224
00:39:24,646 --> 00:39:28,106
output, or also turn that into a point cloud, all to account for the

225
00:39:28,146 --> 00:39:30,126
heterogeneous underlying data.

226
00:39:32,806 --> 00:39:36,626
In terms of future plans, we plan to add more data sources to the Cubing

227
00:39:36,686 --> 00:39:39,086
Engine. That's one of the key prints that we want to do.

228
00:39:39,126 --> 00:39:43,006
That will still go under the banner of the BMD project because plenty of more

229
00:39:43,026 --> 00:39:44,126
data sources are needed.

230
00:39:45,326 --> 00:39:47,226
We plan to publish that as a package as well.

231
00:39:47,266 --> 00:39:50,906
That will probably happen after the refactor from GEO has been done.

232
00:39:51,626 --> 00:39:52,106
And

233
00:39:53,106 --> 00:39:56,706
yes, and not least, we also plan to deploy it as an

234
00:39:56,746 --> 00:40:00,326
internal service in the Biodiversity Data Project so the different analytical

235
00:40:00,386 --> 00:40:02,826
tools can access it through an API.

236
00:40:03,766 --> 00:40:07,686
The API also governs the waiting time and leads them to the outputs where it is

237
00:40:07,766 --> 00:40:10,066
stored, so it can be easily streamed from there.

238
00:40:11,666 --> 00:40:12,386
I think that was

239
00:40:13,226 --> 00:40:16,906
it in a nutshell. There's some links here at the bottom,

