01:38:31,656 --> 01:38:34,776
realm, but there's also another important one, which is the aerial realm, and that

1278
01:38:34,836 --> 01:38:38,756
is what I'm going to talk about. So while we all had, I hope, a good

1279
01:38:38,796 --> 01:38:42,596
night's sleep, there were millions of songbirds migrating over

1280
01:38:42,656 --> 01:38:45,516
us south. And they do this

1281
01:38:47,056 --> 01:38:50,796
mainly at night, and they often do this at very high altitudes

1282
01:38:51,256 --> 01:38:54,436
in massive numbers and in a very broad scale.

1283
01:38:54,716 --> 01:38:58,656
You have some choke points, but they typically do this in a very broad front

1284
01:38:58,716 --> 01:39:02,456
all over the continent. And that makes it difficult to monitor.

1285
01:39:03,376 --> 01:39:07,136
You can use citizen science observations, but those are mainly coming from

1286
01:39:07,636 --> 01:39:11,556
the birds coming back to the ground to roost, or

1287
01:39:11,616 --> 01:39:15,556
you can use GPS trackers, but then you can only track a number of individuals,

1288
01:39:15,636 --> 01:39:17,936
and it's also harder with songbirds, which are smaller.

1289
01:39:19,316 --> 01:39:23,156
But luckily, we have weather radars, and there is a network of weather

1290
01:39:23,216 --> 01:39:26,496
radars that is across the whole continent.

1291
01:39:26,536 --> 01:39:30,036
It is monitoring the sky 24/7. And we mainly do this,

1292
01:39:30,116 --> 01:39:32,116
meteorological institutes do this, to know

1293
01:39:32,916 --> 01:39:35,996
what the weather is going to be like, so to monitor precipitation.

1294
01:39:36,476 --> 01:39:40,336
And the way a radar works is it emits a microwave into the sky at

1295
01:39:40,416 --> 01:39:44,156
different angles, and they do this up to 35 kilometers from the

1296
01:39:44,216 --> 01:39:48,016
radar. And then if an object in the sky, whether it's

1297
01:39:48,096 --> 01:39:51,016
rain, snow, dust, or animals,

1298
01:39:51,976 --> 01:39:55,876
some of those microwaves are bounced back in all different directions, and some of

1299
01:39:55,916 --> 01:39:59,816
those are received back by the radar, which then knows, "Oh, there are

1300
01:39:59,876 --> 01:40:02,016
these objects in the sky." So,

1301
01:40:02,996 --> 01:40:06,736
this is a very useful tool. What you see here are not all the weather radars in

1302
01:40:06,796 --> 01:40:10,736
Europe. There's more. But these are the ones that we have access to.

1303
01:40:11,496 --> 01:40:14,176
And why would we want to have access to weather radars?

1304
01:40:14,576 --> 01:40:17,916
And that is because, and I think this is so cool, weather radars can actually

1305
01:40:17,956 --> 01:40:21,776
detect animals in the sky as well. So this is mainly useful or

1306
01:40:21,836 --> 01:40:24,376
applicable for birds. Insects are a lot harder.

1307
01:40:24,776 --> 01:40:28,616
Bats, too, unless there's really big roosts coming out

1308
01:40:28,676 --> 01:40:32,356
of a cave, for example. Then you can detect those on weather radar.

1309
01:40:32,836 --> 01:40:34,536
But what you can see in the image up there,

1310
01:40:36,356 --> 01:40:39,596
basically, you have to think of this radar as looking in different angles.

1311
01:40:39,636 --> 01:40:43,336
So you see these donut shapes of things that were at different altitude

1312
01:40:43,396 --> 01:40:47,136
layers in the sky. And you can differentiate those

1313
01:40:47,196 --> 01:40:51,076
from precipitation because the birds

1314
01:40:51,476 --> 01:40:55,316
typically have a more variant speed and direction than the rain, which

1315
01:40:55,376 --> 01:40:58,876
is typically going into a more uniform direction, and also the

1316
01:40:58,916 --> 01:41:02,896
reflectivity of animals in the sky is typically lower than rain

1317
01:41:02,916 --> 01:41:05,296
and precipitation. And through that,

1318
01:41:06,156 --> 01:41:09,836
through algorithms that were developed by a community, the Volto Bird

1319
01:41:09,916 --> 01:41:13,696
algorithm can extract that animal information

1320
01:41:13,756 --> 01:41:15,316
from weather radar data. And

1321
01:41:16,316 --> 01:41:20,076
we then can know the altitude at which animals were

1322
01:41:20,136 --> 01:41:24,056
flying, the speed, the direction, and also

1323
01:41:24,116 --> 01:41:26,016
the density or the amount of animals.

1324
01:41:26,416 --> 01:41:29,836
You basically translate the reflectivity that you get back into an

1325
01:41:29,956 --> 01:41:33,896
average size of a songbird, and then you can apply numbers to

1326
01:41:33,956 --> 01:41:37,336
those. These are the amount of birds that were in the sky at this altitude.

1327
01:41:37,836 --> 01:41:40,276
What we do not know is species information.

1328
01:41:40,536 --> 01:41:43,076
It is basically we know there's meat in the sky.

1329
01:41:45,636 --> 01:41:49,556
And through this technology, there's a whole community that has started,

1330
01:41:49,636 --> 01:41:51,936
the radar aviary ecology community.

1331
01:41:52,776 --> 01:41:56,376
And this group of people is basically using radar to study animals

1332
01:41:56,436 --> 01:41:59,936
aloft. And not only through weather radar, but also through more

1333
01:42:00,036 --> 01:42:03,856
specialized radars as vertical-looking radars, which are

1334
01:42:03,956 --> 01:42:05,776
very local but can detect

1335
01:42:07,176 --> 01:42:07,576
very

1336
01:42:08,535 --> 01:42:12,136
precise also insects, and sometimes they can also detect through

1337
01:42:12,376 --> 01:42:16,196
Doppler effect, the wind beat frequency, which then can be translated into

1338
01:42:16,256 --> 01:42:19,616
species groups. Or tracking radars, which are more like the

1339
01:42:20,276 --> 01:42:23,736
military radars, but you track animals rather than aircraft, for example.

1340
01:42:26,236 --> 01:42:27,856
This community has

1341
01:42:28,816 --> 01:42:32,476
been going on for about 10 years and matured and

1342
01:42:32,956 --> 01:42:36,796
has developed a number of things, and one of them is just like that is a data

1343
01:42:36,856 --> 01:42:39,336
standard. So we have the VPTS CSV file,

1344
01:42:40,616 --> 01:42:41,876
or format, which is

1345
01:42:42,696 --> 01:42:46,556
a format to express the time series of what we call vertical

1346
01:42:46,656 --> 01:42:50,096
profiles. So you have altitude layers of animal

1347
01:42:50,136 --> 01:42:53,936
migration, and we use the frictionless table

1348
01:42:54,016 --> 01:42:57,916
schemas to define the fields that you can find, which is direction,

1349
01:42:58,136 --> 01:43:01,916
speeds, the radar location, and also the types and

1350
01:43:01,976 --> 01:43:05,956
constraints of those fields. We also have a similar format to express the

1351
01:43:08,276 --> 01:43:12,256
raw polar volume data that is coming from the radar, as well as

1352
01:43:12,316 --> 01:43:13,216
we have a list of

1353
01:43:14,036 --> 01:43:17,876
the weather radars in Europe and the metadata for those, the status,

1354
01:43:17,996 --> 01:43:20,496
which is co-maintained by the meteorological community.

1355
01:43:20,936 --> 01:43:24,896
But it's especially important for us that we know if a radar has

1356
01:43:24,956 --> 01:43:28,556
changed codes, that it is actually the same radar at the same location, but, for

1357
01:43:28,616 --> 01:43:32,516
example, with a new upgrade, so we can build longer time series with codes

1358
01:43:32,556 --> 01:43:35,056
that have changed so we can tie those together.

1359
01:43:36,676 --> 01:43:40,036
And the other thing we have started in 2017 is a-

1360
01:43:40,628 --> 01:43:44,108
Bucket, the Aloft data portal, where we can

1361
01:43:44,848 --> 01:43:47,128
store this vertical profile data.

1362
01:43:48,567 --> 01:43:51,968
The meteorological institutes have been doing slowly a good job to actually

1363
01:43:52,328 --> 01:43:55,988
open up radar data and not only keep it under a license.

1364
01:43:57,208 --> 01:44:01,028
But they typically keep this data only for two days, so you only have

1365
01:44:01,108 --> 01:44:04,408
access to the data of now and not historical data.

1366
01:44:04,988 --> 01:44:08,928
But to be able to do actually real science over a long time period, we

1367
01:44:08,948 --> 01:44:11,048
have started to archive the

1368
01:44:12,908 --> 01:44:16,168
derived biological data from this weather radar data.

1369
01:44:16,428 --> 01:44:17,168
And so this is a

1370
01:44:18,328 --> 01:44:21,408
data repository over 24 European countries,

1371
01:44:21,768 --> 01:44:25,668
173 radar locations, and we've also filled up the

1372
01:44:25,708 --> 01:44:28,548
historical data until 2012.

1373
01:44:29,608 --> 01:44:33,508
And this is a time series of almost close to 5,000 days.

1374
01:44:33,608 --> 01:44:35,588
So it's a very long time series that we have.

1375
01:44:36,768 --> 01:44:40,748
We've also archived this on Zenodo under CC0 license, and the

1376
01:44:40,788 --> 01:44:42,988
really cool thing is that this is updated daily.

1377
01:44:43,068 --> 01:44:46,148
So as data are coming in, it's also added to this repository,

1378
01:44:46,948 --> 01:44:49,848
with a time delay of about one to two days.

1379
01:44:50,848 --> 01:44:51,008
And

1380
01:44:53,508 --> 01:44:57,448
the last component is there's a number of open source packages that have

1381
01:44:57,488 --> 01:45:01,268
developed to, one, explore different sources of

1382
01:45:01,308 --> 01:45:04,448
radar data and biological data derived from radar that are out there,

1383
01:45:05,208 --> 01:45:06,188
and to

1384
01:45:07,028 --> 01:45:10,948
visualize those and analyze those, as well as the default

1385
01:45:10,988 --> 01:45:14,848
to bird algorithm. You can run it yourself too if you so want to.

1386
01:45:15,468 --> 01:45:18,748
And there is a tool called CRO, which allows us to

1387
01:45:18,828 --> 01:45:22,428
visualize in real-time or semi-real time, the

1388
01:45:22,468 --> 01:45:25,088
migration that is happening now. And this is what I can show you here.

1389
01:45:25,488 --> 01:45:27,088
So this is from a couple of days ago.

1390
01:45:27,248 --> 01:45:29,908
This is a radar about 20 kilometers south from Oslo.

1391
01:45:30,328 --> 01:45:34,168
And what you see on the top chart is the amount of birds that are flying

1392
01:45:34,288 --> 01:45:34,568
over

1393
01:45:37,728 --> 01:45:38,368
a line of

1394
01:45:39,248 --> 01:45:39,928
one kilometer.

1395
01:45:41,168 --> 01:45:45,128
And you can see at the bottom of the chart, the darker colors, so this

1396
01:45:45,208 --> 01:45:45,368
is

1397
01:45:46,248 --> 01:45:50,207
dusk and then night and then dawn, that the birds are really,

1398
01:45:50,308 --> 01:45:51,868
the numbers are really increasing.

1399
01:45:51,968 --> 01:45:55,728
So they stay inactive during the day, but at night, it's really

1400
01:45:55,828 --> 01:45:59,068
ramping up, and you see this very high peak in the morning.

1401
01:45:59,928 --> 01:46:03,568
And on the bottom chart, you see not the amount of birds but

1402
01:46:03,648 --> 01:46:07,608
the height at which this bird flies, and the brighter colors,

1403
01:46:07,648 --> 01:46:11,128
there's a higher density. And as you can see here, they're flying up to

1404
01:46:12,028 --> 01:46:14,288
2,000 meters high, and then they're settling down here.

1405
01:46:15,108 --> 01:46:18,888
And then the next night, we don't have a lot of information because it was

1406
01:46:18,948 --> 01:46:22,208
raining that night, and that's actually good for us that the birds don't really

1407
01:46:22,268 --> 01:46:23,328
move a lot during the rain,

1408
01:46:24,148 --> 01:46:27,888
because it's also much harder for us to differentiate birds from rain

1409
01:46:28,388 --> 01:46:32,008
and radar data that has a lot of rain contamination, we call it.

1410
01:46:32,868 --> 01:46:36,848
This is a clearer picture from Karlskrona in the

1411
01:46:37,248 --> 01:46:40,848
coast of Sweden, and here you can really see these nice blooms of

1412
01:46:40,888 --> 01:46:44,628
density and these high peaks. And if you follow this, you can actually also see the

1413
01:46:44,688 --> 01:46:48,628
live data the night of the 21st of September over

1414
01:46:48,688 --> 01:46:51,988
the Oslo radar, had a very nice peak of

1415
01:46:52,628 --> 01:46:53,788
passerines flying over.

1416
01:46:54,908 --> 01:46:58,728
So how do we evolve from here? There's a lot of potential here,

1417
01:46:58,788 --> 01:47:02,328
there's data there, to something like a digital twin of the aerial

1418
01:47:02,388 --> 01:47:02,988
habitat.

1419
01:47:04,048 --> 01:47:07,468
And I think the main thing why such a twin would be useful is

1420
01:47:07,528 --> 01:47:11,228
migration forecasting, so that you can have models running on the

1421
01:47:11,248 --> 01:47:15,048
historical data to be able to predict what kind of migration are we going to

1422
01:47:15,108 --> 01:47:18,708
have in the future, which is really useful for bird collision

1423
01:47:18,748 --> 01:47:22,388
prevention. So birds often collide with wind

1424
01:47:22,448 --> 01:47:26,408
turbines, but also with aircraft, and the first is dangerous for the birds, but the

1425
01:47:26,468 --> 01:47:29,728
second can be very dangerous for the passengers of the aircraft as well.

1426
01:47:29,788 --> 01:47:33,668
So it is very useful that we can predict when there's going to be massive migration

1427
01:47:33,728 --> 01:47:37,428
of birds. And radar is already used for that

1428
01:47:38,188 --> 01:47:41,708
for larger birds, but also for passerines, it would be very useful.

1429
01:47:42,208 --> 01:47:46,028
The other one would be disease threat modeling. Avian flu, for example.

1430
01:47:46,088 --> 01:47:46,768
If you know where

1431
01:47:47,668 --> 01:47:51,628
waterfowl is going to move, and we can differentiate that from the radar data,

1432
01:47:52,028 --> 01:47:53,168
that would be really useful.

1433
01:47:54,048 --> 01:47:57,967
And it's also very cool for public outreach, and it's, I think,

1434
01:47:58,027 --> 01:48:01,847
an important essential biodiversity variable, the biomass that is flowing over a

1435
01:48:01,888 --> 01:48:02,467
continent.

1436
01:48:03,768 --> 01:48:05,248
But the challenges we have is,

1437
01:48:06,308 --> 01:48:09,828
for meteorologists, these birds are clutter in the data.

1438
01:48:09,868 --> 01:48:12,068
They used to call them angels in World War II.

1439
01:48:12,108 --> 01:48:15,268
It's like things that they see on the radar data that cannot really explain.

1440
01:48:15,728 --> 01:48:18,308
And it's still the case. Some are interested, but they typically

1441
01:48:19,308 --> 01:48:21,988
don't want the biological data and the radar data.

1442
01:48:22,048 --> 01:48:25,888
They want the precipitation, and they also have limited funds, so they

1443
01:48:25,948 --> 01:48:29,308
tend to filter the data and then only publish the filtered data, while we

1444
01:48:29,888 --> 01:48:31,128
want the unfiltered data.

1445
01:48:32,308 --> 01:48:35,448
The other thing is the taxonomic information. Well, you don't have much.

1446
01:48:35,688 --> 01:48:39,368
It's meat in the sky. But if you combine it with, for example,

1447
01:48:40,028 --> 01:48:43,788
citizen science data on the grounds, you are able to know, oh, we had a

1448
01:48:43,868 --> 01:48:47,608
huge flux of migration, then you can actually know, oh, and these were suspicious

1449
01:48:47,648 --> 01:48:50,048
composition that is very likely to be in there.

1450
01:48:50,668 --> 01:48:54,368
And then the typical thing is, this is done by a small community,

1451
01:48:54,528 --> 01:48:58,148
so building and maintaining that infrastructure and scaling is a huge

1452
01:48:58,208 --> 01:48:58,648
challenge.

1453
01:48:59,968 --> 01:49:03,268
In the US, they have a more unified weather radar network.

1454
01:49:03,308 --> 01:49:05,668
It's only done by one organization, NOAA.

1455
01:49:06,308 --> 01:49:10,008
They've been able to build on the historical data that's also been open longer,

1456
01:49:10,568 --> 01:49:13,488
models that can actually predict bird migration.

1457
01:49:13,568 --> 01:49:16,408
It's called BirdCast, and users can go there.

1458
01:49:16,708 --> 01:49:20,548
This is a picture from the night of September 21, but you can also now

1459
01:49:20,588 --> 01:49:24,388
go to 25, 26 of September, so they predict three days ahead.

1460
01:49:24,948 --> 01:49:27,728
And you see this flow, this bloom of migration.

1461
01:49:27,788 --> 01:49:31,548
So they say, "Yeah, there's going to be about 340 million birds flying over

1462
01:49:31,588 --> 01:49:34,888
continental US tonight," which is insane.

1463
01:49:35,068 --> 01:49:38,868
But that's also when birders know, okay, it's really useful to go

1464
01:49:38,948 --> 01:49:42,308
out now, or the next night it's going to be rain, and we predict there's going to

1465
01:49:42,328 --> 01:49:43,228
be less migration.

1466
01:49:44,208 --> 01:49:47,668
So we hope to get there at some point with the European weather radar as well.

1467
01:49:48,960 --> 01:49:51,560
And yeah, I think the take home message for me is

1468
01:49:52,520 --> 01:49:54,840
weather radars can detect birds. It is really cool.

1469
01:49:55,200 --> 01:49:57,920
We already have a network that is out where we don't need to deploy anything.

1470
01:49:57,980 --> 01:50:01,760
The sensors are already out there, and I think it's a

1471
01:50:01,840 --> 01:50:04,480
very unique type of data that they capture.

1472
01:50:04,680 --> 01:50:08,520
And really, the strength comes from combining this with other digital

1473
01:50:08,580 --> 01:50:11,880
twins and other information. It doesn't really make sense for me to publish

1474
01:50:12,540 --> 01:50:16,280
1.5 billion animals

1475
01:50:17,100 --> 01:50:18,460
on GBIF as a data set. It's

1476
01:50:19,780 --> 01:50:22,700
just meat in the sky that doesn't belong on GBIF.

1477
01:50:22,760 --> 01:50:26,680
But when combining with other sources, we can do really, really cool things.

1478
01:50:27,180 --> 01:50:27,580
Thank you.

1479
01:50:36,200 --> 01:50:38,820
Thank you. Do we have a-- Yeah. Dimitri has a question.

1480
01:50:39,880 --> 01:50:41,740
Yes. Thank you very much. Very nice talk.

1481
01:50:41,960 --> 01:50:45,300
I was thinking about this a lot in the context of insect movement, and there is a

1482
01:50:45,340 --> 01:50:47,220
whole field of movement ecology where

1483
01:50:48,280 --> 01:50:52,260
particular pattern of an individual of a group of animals can

1484
01:50:52,300 --> 01:50:56,040
be recognized. And I'm not a birder, but I can tell a flock of geese from a

1485
01:50:56,080 --> 01:50:59,580
murmuration of starlings. So if a human can do it, then

1486
01:50:59,640 --> 01:51:03,080
computers, too. Is there any movement recognition patterns

1487
01:51:03,280 --> 01:51:07,220
potential in moving from meat

1488
01:51:07,260 --> 01:51:09,500
in the sky to species names?

1489
01:51:10,160 --> 01:51:13,780
Not to a species name, but to differentiate insects from

1490
01:51:13,860 --> 01:51:15,580
waterfowl to passerines.

1491
01:51:16,500 --> 01:51:20,080
People are working on this and using AI models to actually differentiate this

1492
01:51:20,120 --> 01:51:20,340
better.

1493
01:51:21,220 --> 01:51:24,760
It is the access to the unfiltered weather data that is one of the bigger

1494
01:51:24,840 --> 01:51:27,880
bottlenecks now to really train those models well. Yeah.

1495
01:51:28,860 --> 01:51:30,400
But watch this space, I would say.

1496
01:51:33,380 --> 01:51:33,760
One more

1497
01:51:38,460 --> 01:51:38,880
question.

