2797
03:37:49,390 --> 03:37:52,870
But I think this is a very important

2798
03:37:52,930 --> 03:37:55,730
standard if you work with media files,

2799
03:37:56,650 --> 03:38:00,110
and I'm sure you do. And I think this is going to

2800
03:38:01,450 --> 03:38:02,410
revolutionize

2801
03:38:03,250 --> 03:38:06,390
how we manage the assets.

2802
03:38:10,070 --> 03:38:12,790
You see here a nice image, which is obviously

2803
03:38:14,990 --> 03:38:15,910
AI-generated.

2804
03:38:18,130 --> 03:38:18,410
So

2805
03:38:19,470 --> 03:38:21,570
this is one of the challenges that we have now,

2806
03:38:23,510 --> 03:38:24,070
because

2807
03:38:24,870 --> 03:38:28,510
if it's not so obvious like this, then you create a lot of

2808
03:38:28,590 --> 03:38:32,290
issues with trust if you start to have these kind of images,

2809
03:38:32,490 --> 03:38:35,930
and you cannot see whether these are actual specimens or

2810
03:38:37,330 --> 03:38:38,130
artificial ones.

2811
03:38:39,750 --> 03:38:41,770
But we have other challenges as well.

2812
03:38:42,910 --> 03:38:45,610
We have unlimited copies of

2813
03:38:46,810 --> 03:38:47,490
media files

2814
03:38:48,350 --> 03:38:51,570
because we do not have these identifiers.

2815
03:38:53,410 --> 03:38:53,570
And

2816
03:38:55,770 --> 03:38:59,330
they are uncoordinated because the metadata and the

2817
03:38:59,370 --> 03:39:00,010
connection

2818
03:39:01,110 --> 03:39:05,030
with other data easily gets lost. If you change the

2819
03:39:05,090 --> 03:39:08,850
name of the file, you already cannot relate that

2820
03:39:08,910 --> 03:39:09,350
back to

2821
03:39:10,370 --> 03:39:11,330
the rest of the data.

2822
03:39:12,790 --> 03:39:16,730
If you have some metadata attached that tells you some provenance of who made

2823
03:39:16,750 --> 03:39:17,350
the image

2824
03:39:18,630 --> 03:39:22,610
and when it was made, it easily becomes disconnected if you change that image from

2825
03:39:22,630 --> 03:39:23,990
a JPEG to a PDF,

2826
03:39:25,350 --> 03:39:27,350
a PNG image, or whatever.

2827
03:39:28,230 --> 03:39:32,210
So uncoordinated copy is an issue and broken link data

2828
03:39:32,270 --> 03:39:32,570
also.

2829
03:39:35,090 --> 03:39:38,130
So why do traditional identifiers fall short

2830
03:39:38,890 --> 03:39:39,770
for images?

2831
03:39:41,210 --> 03:39:45,150
We have traditional persistent identifiers like DOIs,

2832
03:39:46,030 --> 03:39:49,910
and they focus on the location of your content.

2833
03:39:50,830 --> 03:39:54,750
And it's a good practice for persistent identifiers to have

2834
03:39:54,790 --> 03:39:56,090
some metadata included

2835
03:39:57,350 --> 03:40:00,310
that makes one persistent identifier

2836
03:40:03,290 --> 03:40:05,790
identifiable as different from another one.

2837
03:40:06,549 --> 03:40:09,270
And that's easy to do for some things like people,

2838
03:40:10,790 --> 03:40:14,430
institutions, specimens, and you have examples

2839
03:40:14,730 --> 03:40:14,910
like

2840
03:40:16,490 --> 03:40:17,870
RORs and ORCIDs,

2841
03:40:19,330 --> 03:40:22,570
and digital specimen DOIs. But for images, it's hard

2842
03:40:23,710 --> 03:40:24,610
because how do you

2843
03:40:25,830 --> 03:40:29,630
describe an image in such a way that you know that it's different from another

2844
03:40:29,690 --> 03:40:33,390
image? You really need the content of that image, what the image

2845
03:40:33,430 --> 03:40:34,110
represents.

2846
03:40:35,510 --> 03:40:36,690
And that's just not there.

2847
03:40:37,890 --> 03:40:40,510
You could try to solve that with adding a hash.

2848
03:40:41,190 --> 03:40:43,650
You could create a hash. A standard hash solution is

2849
03:40:44,430 --> 03:40:47,090
SHA-2056.

2850
03:40:48,230 --> 03:40:51,750
But the problem with these hashes is that if you change

2851
03:40:52,290 --> 03:40:56,270
the image even a tiny little bit, even if you change one pixel, but

2852
03:40:56,350 --> 03:40:59,830
also if you change the metadata or you change the format of the image,

2853
03:41:00,630 --> 03:41:04,550
you already generate a completely different hash, and you cannot relate

2854
03:41:04,570 --> 03:41:05,590
these things to each other.

2855
03:41:07,990 --> 03:41:11,170
And this is what the International Standard Content Code

2856
03:41:11,770 --> 03:41:12,210
solves.

2857
03:41:13,430 --> 03:41:14,810
So this is really great, I think.

2858
03:41:16,630 --> 03:41:19,010
It can work on images, on

2859
03:41:20,350 --> 03:41:22,570
video, on audio, and even on text.

2860
03:41:24,330 --> 03:41:26,590
And it is a code that has four

2861
03:41:27,590 --> 03:41:28,330
different parts.

2862
03:41:30,350 --> 03:41:34,050
You can also have codes for only one of these four, but you can also have codes

2863
03:41:34,090 --> 03:41:36,130
that have all these four different parts.

2864
03:41:38,390 --> 03:41:42,270
One is a code for the metadata

2865
03:41:42,370 --> 03:41:43,090
of the media.

2866
03:41:44,110 --> 03:41:47,530
One is for the content, for what it represents.

2867
03:41:48,650 --> 03:41:52,470
One is a code for the data, and one is a code for the instance.

2868
03:41:52,970 --> 03:41:56,810
Now, the instance is similar to the hash, so if there's a little change, the image

2869
03:41:57,310 --> 03:42:00,970
code changes. The data code only changes

2870
03:42:01,170 --> 03:42:05,070
if there is a change in the data, but not a

2871
03:42:05,130 --> 03:42:08,950
change in the content. So if you change a metadata, if you

2872
03:42:09,030 --> 03:42:09,930
add a watermark,

2873
03:42:11,950 --> 03:42:15,930
if you change the format from a JPEG to a PNG, these

2874
03:42:15,970 --> 03:42:19,750
kind of things, your data code will change, but your content

2875
03:42:19,790 --> 03:42:23,510
code will still be the same because what the image represents is still

2876
03:42:23,570 --> 03:42:23,850
there.

2877
03:42:25,810 --> 03:42:27,310
And they use some intelligent

2878
03:42:28,510 --> 03:42:31,690
algorithms for that, SimHash and things like that.

2879
03:42:32,990 --> 03:42:36,690
And what's unique also in these codes is that you can use

2880
03:42:36,790 --> 03:42:37,650
Hamming distance.

2881
03:42:39,010 --> 03:42:39,230
So

2882
03:42:40,950 --> 03:42:44,730
usually you say if you have a persistent identifier, you don't want any semantics

2883
03:42:44,790 --> 03:42:48,530
in it. This carries semantics. And I think it's

2884
03:42:48,590 --> 03:42:51,330
fine because it's identifying the content itself.

2885
03:42:52,970 --> 03:42:56,730
And that means that if you compare two identifiers,

2886
03:42:57,930 --> 03:43:01,570
you can look at how many bytes they are different.

2887
03:43:02,850 --> 03:43:03,270
And

2888
03:43:04,110 --> 03:43:04,570
I see that.

2889
03:43:07,830 --> 03:43:11,450
And so you can calculate how much these two

2890
03:43:11,510 --> 03:43:13,110
images are different.

2891
03:43:14,450 --> 03:43:18,350
And you can do that for the content, but you can also do that for the

2892
03:43:18,390 --> 03:43:19,070
data itself.

2893
03:43:23,090 --> 03:43:25,730
So this allows smart matching of images.

2894
03:43:25,790 --> 03:43:29,610
You can match images only on the content, and you can compare them, and you can

2895
03:43:29,670 --> 03:43:30,890
find similar images.

2896
03:43:35,950 --> 03:43:37,190
A short breakdown.

2897
03:43:38,150 --> 03:43:39,030
So we have these

2898
03:43:42,650 --> 03:43:42,930
four

2899
03:43:44,850 --> 03:43:47,250
parts of the code.

2900
03:43:48,950 --> 03:43:49,850
The code is

2901
03:43:50,910 --> 03:43:54,769
a concatenation of these four parts, plus some

2902
03:43:55,290 --> 03:43:58,810
self-describing metadata about the code.

2903
03:43:59,730 --> 03:44:03,530
The main type describes whether it contains all the four parts or

2904
03:44:03,590 --> 03:44:04,910
only one of them.

2905
03:44:05,750 --> 03:44:08,130
The subtype describes whether it's an image or

2906
03:44:09,110 --> 03:44:12,590
a sound because you need different algorithms for the content

2907
03:44:12,630 --> 03:44:13,290
detection.

2908
03:44:15,310 --> 03:44:16,790
And it's usually

2909
03:44:18,130 --> 03:44:21,850
using 64 bits for each part, but you can also have a

2910
03:44:21,890 --> 03:44:22,690
shortened code.

2911
03:44:26,490 --> 03:44:26,770
So

2912
03:44:28,170 --> 03:44:31,410
if we have this, which is great, what are we then still missing?

2913
03:44:32,190 --> 03:44:33,190
What is still missing,

2914
03:44:34,150 --> 03:44:38,010
and that's something that's still being worked on, so this is in

2915
03:44:38,050 --> 03:44:40,430
beta. It's not a part yet of the standard.

2916
03:44:42,150 --> 03:44:43,350
You want to bind

2917
03:44:44,370 --> 03:44:48,210
this ICC code, the content code, with

2918
03:44:48,270 --> 03:44:51,990
who created that content and when was it created.

2919
03:44:52,830 --> 03:44:56,450
And this is what they do with the declaration event, which

2920
03:44:56,510 --> 03:44:58,110
is a FAIR digital object

2921
03:44:59,210 --> 03:45:02,730
that binds the ICC code together with

2922
03:45:03,310 --> 03:45:05,630
a signature of who created the content

2923
03:45:06,490 --> 03:45:08,350
and the timestamp.

2924
03:45:09,730 --> 03:45:13,670
They started to do this with a blockchain-like ledger, but now I think they just

2925
03:45:13,790 --> 03:45:14,010
use

2926
03:45:17,090 --> 03:45:19,470
a log that's append only. So

2927
03:45:21,030 --> 03:45:23,750
you don't need to have a blockchain anymore.

2928
03:45:24,310 --> 03:45:25,990
And they use DIDs,

2929
03:45:26,970 --> 03:45:28,750
decentralized identifiers,

2930
03:45:29,950 --> 03:45:32,530
and a peer-to-peer-like solution.

2931
03:45:32,590 --> 03:45:33,610
So everybody can

2932
03:45:34,810 --> 03:45:37,490
run a server, run a hub. The

2933
03:45:38,630 --> 03:45:42,410
source code is available. You can just run it locally, and you can

2934
03:45:43,430 --> 03:45:46,170
run an index with these declaration events.

2935
03:45:47,202 --> 03:45:50,962
For the codes themselves, you can also run the algorithms just on your local

2936
03:45:51,002 --> 03:45:54,602
laptop. You can generate the algorithm of the content so anybody

2937
03:45:54,682 --> 03:45:58,482
can create or recreate the ICC code

2938
03:45:58,582 --> 03:45:59,842
from the content.

2939
03:46:03,802 --> 03:46:07,742
So this enables the content-based discovery and also the

2940
03:46:07,782 --> 03:46:11,402
provenance, so who created the image, and this also isolates

2941
03:46:11,502 --> 03:46:15,202
AI-generated fakes because the AI

2942
03:46:15,282 --> 03:46:18,722
fakes will never have these signatures of who created it.

2943
03:46:23,762 --> 03:46:27,282
It is also creating resilience for annotations,

2944
03:46:27,582 --> 03:46:28,182
because

2945
03:46:30,802 --> 03:46:34,582
if you have annotations, you can link them not to

2946
03:46:34,962 --> 03:46:37,002
the image file, but you can link them

2947
03:46:37,862 --> 03:46:39,782
to the ICC ID.

2948
03:46:40,622 --> 03:46:41,462
And that means that

2949
03:46:42,782 --> 03:46:43,402
even if

2950
03:46:44,642 --> 03:46:48,562
you have an image that's a little bit different, it is a derivative from

2951
03:46:48,602 --> 03:46:49,442
the original image,

2952
03:46:50,322 --> 03:46:50,662
you can

2953
03:46:52,982 --> 03:46:56,142
still keep the annotations linked to the content

2954
03:46:56,922 --> 03:46:58,582
because the content is still the same.

